1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,380
Okay, welcome back.

2
00:00:01,380 --> 00:00:05,160
We're now going to begin Chapter 3, Principles of Neoplasia.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:08,640
And the first section in this particular chapter

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,840
is actually entitled Neoplasia.

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,280
Before we begin, just some basic principles.

6
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:18,840
The word neoplasia, neo means new, and plasia means growth.

7
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:22,080
And what's important to recognize concerning neoplasia

8
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:28,520
is that it is unregulated, irreversible, and monoclonal.

9
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,040
Now these three features are important

10
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:31,560
because it's these three features,

11
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:34,420
the fact that the growth is unregulated,

12
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:37,300
that it's irreversible, and that it's monoclonal.

13
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:40,120
These three features help to distinguish neoplasia

14
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,680
from hyperplasia or repair.

15
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:46,100
Now just as an example, hyperplasia, as you'll recall,

16
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,700
occurred when there might be an increase in stress

17
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:50,820
on an organ.

18
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:53,140
For example, a woman becomes pregnant,

19
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:57,080
the uterus, the smooth muscle of the uterine wall

20
00:00:57,080 --> 00:00:59,080
actually undergoes hyperplasia.

21
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:01,100
However, hyperplasia is regulated.

22
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:04,320
It's regulated by the physiology of the pregnancy.

23
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:05,220
It's reversible.

24
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:10,060
When the woman delivers, her uterus will again shrink down

25
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:12,880
to normal size, and it's polyclonal.

26
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:17,300
And by polyclonal, what we mean is that multiple cells

27
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:21,980
produce daughter cells, which then subsequently results

28
00:01:21,980 --> 00:01:23,580
in the hyperplasia.

29
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:25,880
Whereas cancer is monoclonal, and we're going to talk

30
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:29,340
about monoclonality in a moment.

31
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,120
So again, these are three very important features

32
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:34,160
of neoplasia.

33
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:36,420
Now as I mentioned just a moment ago,

34
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:40,920
monoclonal refers to the fact that all the tumor cells

35
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:44,420
actually arise from one single progenitor cell,

36
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:47,740
one single mother cell, or one single father cell.

37
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:49,720
And that's what the term monoclonal means.

38
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:52,880
So as I've stated here, the neoplastic cells are derived

39
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:56,100
from a single mother cell.

40
00:01:56,100 --> 00:02:01,180
Clonality can be determined by G6PD isoforms,

41
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:04,520
and this actually is a very high-yield principle,

42
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:07,140
which we should probably take a moment to highlight.

43
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:10,120
So what I'm going to do here is actually just create a whiteboard,

44
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:13,620
and then spend a moment just explaining what we mean by this.

45
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:20,120
So let's go back and pretend for a moment that we are looking

46
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:22,080
at the uterus of a female.

47
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:26,580
And let's just imagine that this uterus actually is comprised

48
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:28,280
of four cells.

49
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:31,580
Now obviously, the female uterus is not made up of four cells,

50
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:36,460
but we can just imagine just for the purposes of illustration.

51
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:40,320
One of the important enzymes that is present on the X chromosome

52
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:43,020
in a female is G6PD.

53
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:46,620
And I'm going to take a moment to remind you that G6PD is actually

54
00:02:46,620 --> 00:02:50,800
X-linked, and that there are multiple isoforms that exist

55
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,460
across the population.

56
00:02:52,460 --> 00:03:00,220
So for example, there may be a G6PD-A, a G6PD-B, a G6PD-C,

57
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:08,920
a G6PD-D, etc. There are dozens of isoforms of G6PD.

58
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:14,680
Now any single female in the population will have only inherited

59
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:18,260
one copy of G6PD, one isoform of G6PD from their mother,

60
00:03:18,260 --> 00:03:21,620
and one isoform of G6PD from their father.

61
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:26,960
So let's pretend a woman inherits G6PD-A from the mother

62
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:32,660
or the father, and G6PD-B from the other parent.

63
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:36,300
So basically, each of the cells in that female,

64
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:39,800
because she's got two X chromosomes and G6PD is X-linked,

65
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:44,120
each of the cells in that female will express both G6PD-A

66
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:45,820
and G6PD-B.

67
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:49,500
So for example, if this uterus that we've drawn up here

68
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:53,060
is actually made up of four cells, then these four cells

69
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:57,420
would be expressing G6PD-A and B, and each of them

70
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:01,900
would express the two isoforms because each cell contains

71
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:04,200
two X chromosomes.

72
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,520
So it would look something like that.

73
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:11,600
Now the next important concept to bear in mind is that

74
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:16,220
in a female, one X chromosome is going to be randomly inactivated,

75
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:19,400
so that in any particular cell there would only be one active

76
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:25,600
variant of G6PD, and you know that the inactivation of the X

77
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:27,260
chromosome is actually random.

78
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:29,700
So for example, if we had these four cells that I've drawn up

79
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:34,040
here, it may be that in this particular cell, G6PD-B is

80
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:36,860
inactivated, or that X chromosome is activated.

81
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:40,800
It may be that in this cell, this X chromosome is inactivated.

82
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:45,200
It may be that in this cell, this X chromosome is inactivated,

83
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:48,200
and that in this cell, this X chromosome is inactivated.

84
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,900
Now X chromosome inactivation is going to be random, so that in

85
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,900
the end, if we were to look at all the cells present within the

86
00:04:55,900 --> 00:05:01,160
female's uterus, for example, the ratio of G6PD-A to G6PD-B

87
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:03,200
would be one to one.

88
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:10,960
Now, let's just imagine for a moment that a woman develops

89
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:14,600
hyperplasia of her uterus.

90
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:19,800
So she becomes pregnant, and the uterine wall undergoes hyperplasia,

91
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:22,960
and when it undergoes hyperplasia, hyperplasia is defined as an

92
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,740
increase in the number of cells.

93
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:28,800
So let's say that, for example, this particular cell produces two

94
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:34,300
daughter cells, and this particular cell produces two daughter cells,

95
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:38,640
and this cell also produces two daughter cells, and this cell also

96
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,500
produces two daughter cells.

97
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:45,800
Now, the daughter cells would show the exact same active isoform of G6PD as

98
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:46,700
their mother cell.

99
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:51,900
So these two cells would produce G6PD, would be expressing G6PD-A, these two

100
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:58,600
cells would be expressing G6PD-B, these two cells would express G6PD-A, and these two

101
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:05,500
cells would express G6PD-B, because obviously the mother cell also expresses G6PD-B.

102
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:13,500
So in hyperplasia, which is polyclonal, i.e., the cells are derived from multiple

103
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:19,000
individual cells, the ratio of G6PD-A is going to be maintained, because here we can

104
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:29,300
see that there are one, two, three, four cells that are expressing G6PD-A, and one, two, three,

105
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:32,500
four cells that are expressing G6PD-B.

106
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:37,800
So really, that maintains the one-to-one ratio, and that's exactly what we would expect in

107
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:43,600
a polyclonal proliferation, i.e., that's what we would expect to see in hyperplasia, that

108
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:47,200
the one-to-one ratio of G6PD is maintained.

109
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:51,500
Now let's take another example, and let's go back and draw the exact same scenario.

110
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:58,000
So again, we've got four cells present within the uterus, and these are the smooth muscle

111
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:04,000
cells of the uterus, and we said that, you know, this is perhaps expressing G6PD-A, this

112
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:10,200
one expresses G6PD-B, this one expresses G6PD-A, and this one expresses G6PD-B, so that the

113
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:13,100
ratio is approximately one-to-one.

114
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:19,200
Now in the case of neoplasia, all the cells, all the tumor cells, are derived from a single

115
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:20,400
mother cell.

116
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:26,400
So for example, if this one particular cell were to become mutated and result in neoplasia,

117
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:32,940
then what would happen is that one cell would actually produce all of the tumor cells.

118
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:39,300
And so all of the tumor cells would actually all express G6PD-A.

119
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:44,900
And so it's this concept that one cell produces all the tumor cells that is actually the idea

120
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:50,600
behind monoclonality, i.e., there is one mother cell that's producing all these baby cells.

121
00:07:50,600 --> 00:08:01,500
And the way by which we can prove monoclonality in a female would be to look at G6PD isoforms.

122
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:08,240
And so if we see that the tumor is composed of one isoform of G6PD, then that would indicate

123
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:13,700
that we're dealing with a monoclonal proliferation, hence we're dealing with neoplasia.

124
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:19,200
Whereas if we saw that the G6PD isoforms were expressed in a roughly one-to-one ratio, then

125
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:23,600
we would know that we're actually dealing with a polyclonal proliferation, which would

126
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:26,200
basically be hyperplasia.

127
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:31,200
So I hope that that's clear, because that's a very high-yield concept that shows up on

128
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:32,200
examinations.

129
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:34,960
And so let's go back and now read what we have here in the text.

130
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:39,300
So we say that one of the key characteristics of tumor is that it is monoclonal.

131
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:43,200
So again, one of the key characteristics of neoplasia is that it is monoclonal.

132
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:47,200
And that means that the neoplastic cells are derived from a single mother cell.

133
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:52,200
And as I've stated here, clonality can be determined by G6PD isoforms.

134
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:57,500
Now we could play the exact same game using androgen receptor isoforms, because androgen

135
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:00,700
receptor isoforms are also on the X chromosome.

136
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:05,700
And so we could say that the patient has androgen receptor A, androgen receptor B, androgen

137
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:08,200
receptor C or D or E, etc.

138
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:11,200
And we could play the exact same game that we played with G6PD.

139
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:15,200
But this is a fundamental principle of neoplasia.

140
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:21,200
And that is that all neoplasia, whether it be benign, whether it be malignant, all neoplasia,

141
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:25,200
all tumors, are monoclonal by definition.

142
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:26,200
Okay.

143
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:33,200
Another way that we could play the clonality game is to look at immunoglobulin light chain

144
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:34,200
phenotype.

145
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:39,200
And this is a way by which we can determine the clonality of B cells.

146
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:44,200
This is high yield, and so it's worth taking a moment to understand the concept.

147
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:49,200
Now let's play the same game, but this time pretend that there is an individual, and we're

148
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:51,200
looking at their lymph node.

149
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:56,200
Now let's pretend that this individual's lymph node is made up of four cells.

150
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:57,200
Okay.

151
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:00,200
Obviously it's not, but for the purposes of illustration, it makes it easy.

152
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:06,200
So if the patient actually has four cells in their lymph node, you should recall that the

153
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:13,200
B cells present within the lymph node express immunoglobulin, and that immunoglobulin is comprised of both

154
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:15,200
heavy chain and light chain.

155
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:21,200
Now the light chain for each individual B cell will either be kappa or lambda.

156
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:23,200
There's only two choices.

157
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:27,200
Either a B cell expresses kappa or it expresses lambda light chain.

158
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:32,200
And generally speaking, the ratio of kappa to lambda cells within the lymph node or within

159
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:35,200
any collection of lymphocytes is three to one.

160
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:42,200
So that if we had four cells making up this lymph node, three of the cells would express kappa

161
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:47,200
light chain, and one of the cells would express lambda light chain.

162
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:48,200
All right.

163
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:52,200
Now let's pretend that a patient gets an infection.

164
00:10:52,200 --> 00:10:59,200
And when they get an infection, what's going to happen is that they're going to get hyperplasia

165
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:05,200
of the cells of the lymph node because there's going to be a response to the infection and

166
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:07,200
the B cells are going to proliferate.

167
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:11,200
And we know that when we get infections that the lymph nodes become bigger.

168
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:16,200
And so if the lymph node becomes bigger, we could ask the question, why is the lymph node bigger?

169
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:18,200
What type of proliferation is occurring?

170
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:23,200
And if there was an infection and the proliferation was due to hyperplasia, then what we would expect

171
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:28,200
is that in hyperplasia, each of these cells would contribute because it's polyclonal.

172
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:31,200
Each of these cells would contribute additional cells.

173
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:41,200
So for example, if this is a kappa light chain B cell, then each of these cells is making additional cells.

174
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,200
Let's just draw these here.

175
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:50,200
And each of the additional cells is going to have the same light chain ratio of three to one.

176
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:53,200
And so, you know, these two would be kappa.

177
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:55,200
These two would be kappa.

178
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:57,200
These two would be kappa.

179
00:11:57,200 --> 00:11:58,200
These would be lambda.

180
00:11:58,200 --> 00:11:59,200
And this would be lambda.

181
00:11:59,200 --> 00:12:08,200
And so the ratio here is one, two, three, four, five, six to two, which really is the same three to one ratio

182
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:14,200
that we would expect to see in a normal lymph node.

183
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:21,200
And again, that's because the proliferation in hyperplasia is polyclonal.

184
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:33,200
And so one of the key features of a polyclonal proliferation is that the kappa to lambda ratio of immunoglobulin light chain would remain three to one.

185
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:38,200
Now, let's take the other extreme, and I'm going to go ahead and erase this.

186
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:41,200
And let's take another extreme where a patient has a lymph node.

187
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:48,200
And the lymph node is basically a kappa, you know, three kappas, as we've already explained.

188
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:51,200
The normal ratio is three to one and one lambda.

189
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:54,200
And then let's pretend that the patient gets a neoplastic proliferation.

190
00:12:54,200 --> 00:13:05,200
Now, when a patient gets a neoplastic proliferation, i.e. a lymphoma, one cell is going to be making all of the malignant cells.

191
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:12,200
And so what's going to happen is we're going to get a massive excess of kappa.

192
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:24,200
And, of course, there will still be some lambdas, but what will happen is that the kappa to lambda ratio, instead of being three to one, will become something like 20 to one.

193
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:28,200
And this is very high yield, and that's exactly the way the examiners play it.

194
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:31,200
They tell you that the patient's got an enlarged lymph node.

195
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:35,200
Okay, when the patient has an enlarged lymph node, what's the differential diagnosis?

196
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:39,200
Well, there's really only three things that you really think about when you have an enlarged lymph node.

197
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:43,200
One thing that can enlarge the lymph node is metastatic cancer.

198
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:47,200
So if a patient has cancer and it spreads to the lymph node, the lymph node becomes bigger.

199
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:54,200
Another thing that can enlarge the lymph node is reactive hyperplasia, because the patient gets an infection and the lymph node becomes bigger.

200
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:57,200
And the third thing that can enlarge the lymph node is going to be lymphoma.

201
00:13:57,200 --> 00:14:08,200
Now, let's say that they tell you that the patient has an enlarged lymph node, a biopsy is performed, and it shows a proliferation of lymphocytes.

202
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:14,200
Then we're thinking, aha, proliferation of lymphocytes, it must be either reactive hyperplasia or lymphoma.

203
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:22,200
And then they'll tell you on an examination that the light chain is studied and the light chain ratio is 20 to one.

204
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:28,200
So once you hear that the light chain ratio is 20 to one, you know that you're dealing with a monoclonal proliferation.

205
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:36,200
And that monoclonal proliferation must then be a lymphoma, because that's really the only tumor that you can get within a lymph node.

206
00:14:36,200 --> 00:14:40,200
So I hope that that's clear because that, again, is also high yield.

207
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:42,200
So again, here's what I've stated in the book.

208
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:48,200
In your text, the clonality of B cells is determined by immunoglobulin light chain phenotype.

209
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:56,200
Okay, now, going back to a general principle, when we have neoplasia, neoplasia can be benign or it can be malignant.

210
00:14:56,200 --> 00:15:01,200
Let me make something very clear, because sometimes this is misunderstood and it's missed on examinations as well.

211
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:09,200
Both benign tumors or neoplasia and malignant tumors or neoplasia, both of these are monoclonal.

212
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:16,200
Whether it be a benign tumor or it be a malignant tumor, neoplasia, generally speaking, is monoclonal.

213
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:18,200
Alright, so I want to highlight that point.

214
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:26,200
Now, benign tumors, one of the key characteristics of benign tumors is that they tend to remain localized in the tissue in which they grow,

215
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:29,200
and they absolutely do not metastasize.

216
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:32,200
That's a key characteristic of benign tumors.

217
00:15:32,200 --> 00:15:39,200
Malignant tumors are also called cancer, and a characteristic of malignant tumors is that they invade locally,

218
00:15:39,200 --> 00:15:42,200
and they have the potential to metastasize.

219
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:44,200
What do I mean by potential to metastasize?

220
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:52,200
Meaning that in a subset of patients, those tumors will metastasize, but it doesn't need to metastasize in every single patient.

221
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:56,200
So, for example, if a woman has breast cancer and it's localized to the breast,

222
00:15:56,200 --> 00:16:02,200
if that cancer has the propensity to metastasize in patients, then we would still call it cancer,

223
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:06,200
even though it hasn't metastasized in that individual patient.

224
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:10,200
So, it simply has the potential to metastasize in any given patient.

225
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:15,200
So, again, this is another general principle concerning neoplasia.

226
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:22,200
Now, if we go a step further, we can go, and given that we understand that tumors can be benign or malignant,

227
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:28,200
we should then understand that tumors will have particular lineages of differentiation.

228
00:16:28,200 --> 00:16:32,200
And the way I think about this is, you know, you've got four major tissues within the body.

229
00:16:32,200 --> 00:16:37,200
You've got epithelium, which are the cells that line the surface of the body.

230
00:16:37,200 --> 00:16:43,200
So, they line the skin, they line the gut, they line the esophagus, they line the urogenital tract.

231
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:45,200
All right, those are epithelial cells.

232
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:46,200
You've got mesenchyme.

233
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:47,200
What is mesenchyme?

234
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:49,200
Mesenchyme is a connective tissue in the body.

235
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:54,200
So, the mesenchyme includes fat, blood vessels, bone, cartilage, okay?

236
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:56,200
It's your soft tissue, basically.

237
00:16:56,200 --> 00:16:58,200
Your soft tissue, your bone, your cartilage.

238
00:16:58,200 --> 00:16:59,200
That's mesenchyme.

239
00:16:59,200 --> 00:17:02,200
There are lymphocytes in the body, and then there are melanocytes in the body.

240
00:17:02,200 --> 00:17:06,200
And each of these individual types of cells can develop tumors,

241
00:17:06,200 --> 00:17:09,200
and when they develop tumors, those tumors can be benign or malignant.

242
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:17,200
So, for example, a benign tumor of epithelium, when it produces glands, is called an adenoma.

243
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:24,200
And a malignant tumor of epithelium, when it produces glands, is called an adenocarcinoma.

244
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:31,200
A benign tumor of epithelium, when it creates papillary finger-like structures, is called a papilloma.

245
00:17:31,200 --> 00:17:38,200
And a malignant tumor of epithelium, when it creates papillary finger-like structures, is called a papillary carcinoma.

246
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:41,200
And what do I mean by papillary finger-like structures?

247
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:46,200
Well, it basically means that you've got this growth of epithelial cells.

248
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:48,200
So these are the epithelial cells.

249
00:17:48,200 --> 00:17:53,200
And that they overlie a connective tissue core with a blood vessel in the center.

250
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:57,200
So there's a connective tissue core with a blood vessel in the center.

251
00:17:57,200 --> 00:17:59,200
And so those are papillary growths.

252
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:01,200
And papillary growths, when they're benign, they're called papillomas.

253
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:04,200
When they're malignant, they're called papillary carcinomas.

254
00:18:04,200 --> 00:18:09,200
Mesenchymal tissue can also be benign or can produce tumors that will be benign or malignant.

255
00:18:09,200 --> 00:18:14,200
And it's going to be named based on the type of tissue that it forms.

256
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:17,200
So lipo means fat, and oma means mass.

257
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:20,200
So a benign tumor of fat is a lipoma.

258
00:18:20,200 --> 00:18:22,200
And lipo, again, means fat.

259
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:25,200
And sarcoma means malignant mesenchymal tissue.

260
00:18:25,200 --> 00:18:30,200
And so a malignant tumor of fat would be called a liposarcoma.

261
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:32,200
And you can play this game with all types of mesenchymal.

262
00:18:32,200 --> 00:18:35,200
For example, osteoma, benign tumor of bone.

263
00:18:35,200 --> 00:18:38,200
Osteosarcoma, malignant tumor of bone.

264
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:42,200
Chondroma, benign tumor of cartilage.

265
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:47,200
Chondrosarcoma, malignant tumor of cartilage.

266
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:51,200
Angioma, benign tumor of blood vessels.

267
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:55,200
Angiosarcoma, malignant tumor of blood vessels.

268
00:18:55,200 --> 00:18:56,200
Etc.

269
00:18:56,200 --> 00:18:57,200
Okay.

270
00:18:57,200 --> 00:19:01,200
Lymphocytes can also form tumors.

271
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:05,200
There is no such thing as a benign tumor of lymphocytes.

272
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:08,200
Anytime you have a neoplastic proliferation of lymphocytes,

273
00:19:08,200 --> 00:19:11,200
it is automatically lymphoma or leukemia.

274
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:12,200
Alright?

275
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:15,200
It automatically becomes lymphoma or leukemia.

276
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:19,200
And melanocytes can also produce tumors.

277
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:22,200
And we can get benign tumors or malignant tumors.

278
00:19:22,200 --> 00:19:25,200
A benign tumor of melanocytes is called a nevus.

279
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:28,200
Also commonly called a mole.

280
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:31,200
And a malignant tumor of melanocytes is called a melanoma.

281
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:34,200
Again, melanoma referring to the melanocyte.

282
00:19:34,200 --> 00:19:37,200
And oma referring to mass.

283
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:42,200
And in fact, that was the basis for my naming of the website

284
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:45,200
that I've put all of these lectures on, Pathoma,

285
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:49,200
i.e. Massive Pathology Information.

286
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:51,200
But anyway, that's a separate discussion.

287
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:53,200
So that's tumor nomenclature.

288
00:19:53,200 --> 00:19:55,200
And we should be familiar with that because we're going to do

289
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:58,200
a lot of tumor study as we move forward.

290
00:19:58,200 --> 00:20:00,200
And you're going to have to understand the nomenclature.

291
00:20:00,200 --> 00:20:05,200
But we will cover it as we go through the organ systems again.

292
00:20:05,200 --> 00:20:08,200
Now, epidemiology is important to understand.

293
00:20:08,200 --> 00:20:11,200
The examiners like to go after epidemiology.

294
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:14,200
I think it's important for you to be familiar with a little bit

295
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:17,200
of epidemiology as you go through your medical training.

296
00:20:17,200 --> 00:20:19,200
And so let's just mention a few important points.

297
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:22,200
First of all, cancer is the second leading cause of death

298
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:25,200
in both adults and children.

299
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:27,200
And it's important to know that, generally speaking,

300
00:20:27,200 --> 00:20:31,200
the major causes of death in adults are, number one,

301
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:33,200
cardiovascular disease.

302
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:36,200
So that probably tells you that cardiovascular disease is important

303
00:20:36,200 --> 00:20:37,200
to be familiar with.

304
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:39,200
You're going to see a ton of it in your careers.

305
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:42,200
And you're going to see a ton of it on examinations.

306
00:20:42,200 --> 00:20:46,200
Cancer is the second cause of death in adults,

307
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:48,200
second leading cause of death in adults.

308
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:50,200
So we should be well aware of cancer,

309
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:52,200
and we're going to see it commonly.

310
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:54,200
And the third is cerebrovascular disease.

311
00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:57,200
And so these are the three leading causes of death in adults.

312
00:20:57,200 --> 00:21:00,200
Causes of death in children, number one, accidents.

313
00:21:00,200 --> 00:21:07,200
And accidents, unfortunately, are a cause of death

314
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:12,200
that can often be avoided but are overlooked.

315
00:21:12,200 --> 00:21:15,200
And so we need to be particularly careful about making sure

316
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:18,200
that we as physicians, when we have access

317
00:21:18,200 --> 00:21:21,200
to a pediatric population, ensure that patients are particularly

318
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,200
careful with their children.

319
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:26,200
For example, making sure that children are in car seats

320
00:21:26,200 --> 00:21:28,200
and wear their seat belts, et cetera.

321
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:30,200
But anyway, accidents are the number one cause

322
00:21:30,200 --> 00:21:31,200
of death in children.

323
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:34,200
The second leading cause of death in children is cancer.

324
00:21:34,200 --> 00:21:40,200
And the third leading cause of death in children is congenital defects.

325
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:44,200
Taking the epidemiology one step further,

326
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:48,200
we can ask ourselves, what are the most common cancers in adults?

327
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:53,200
Now these types of tables in which the most common cancers in adults

328
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:58,200
are discussed, basically what you should know is that these exclude skin.

329
00:21:58,200 --> 00:22:02,200
So squamous cell carcinoma and basal cell carcinoma of the skin

330
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:04,200
are very common tumors.

331
00:22:04,200 --> 00:22:08,200
But we're going to exclude them because although they're malignant,

332
00:22:08,200 --> 00:22:12,200
they rarely metastasize and they're more of a nuisance than anything else.

333
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:15,200
They quickly reveal themselves because they're on the skin

334
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:18,200
and they can easily be treated and are rarely,

335
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:20,200
or are not commonly a cause of death.

336
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:23,200
And so basically we're going to remove those tumors from the list

337
00:22:23,200 --> 00:22:26,200
and we're going to talk about the most common cancers in adults

338
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:28,200
by incidence and by mortality.

339
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:32,200
So the three most common cancers in adults by incidence are,

340
00:22:32,200 --> 00:22:34,200
number one, breast or prostate.

341
00:22:34,200 --> 00:22:37,200
Obviously breast in a female and prostate in a male.

342
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:39,200
Number two, lung.

343
00:22:39,200 --> 00:22:42,200
That's the second most common cancer by incidence.

344
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:44,200
And number three, colorectal.

345
00:22:44,200 --> 00:22:48,200
So these are the sort of the most common cancers by incidence.

346
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:52,200
Now if we were to look at this list by mortality,

347
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:54,200
by those cancers that actually cause death,

348
00:22:54,200 --> 00:22:56,200
then lung jumps up to number one.

349
00:22:56,200 --> 00:23:01,200
And then breast in a female and prostate in a male is number two.

350
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:04,200
And colorectal is number three.

351
00:23:04,200 --> 00:23:10,200
And so one of the things that I like to highlight when we look at this list

352
00:23:10,200 --> 00:23:12,200
is notice the change.

353
00:23:12,200 --> 00:23:15,200
Although breast and prostate are the most common,

354
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:18,200
they don't, they aren't the most common killers.

355
00:23:18,200 --> 00:23:20,200
And instead lung,

356
00:23:20,200 --> 00:23:23,200
although it's less common than breast and prostate,

357
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:25,200
jumps up to become the number one killer.

358
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:28,200
And I, and I'd like to, like to highlight that

359
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:30,200
because it's an important fact to be aware of,

360
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:32,200
in particular because lung cancer,

361
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:35,200
the vast majority of it is actually due to smoking.

362
00:23:35,200 --> 00:23:38,200
And this is the one cancer on the list where we exactly,

363
00:23:38,200 --> 00:23:41,200
we have a very, very good idea of what causes it.

364
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:45,200
And it's a relatively removable cause

365
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:48,200
because although it might be difficult to stop smoking,

366
00:23:48,200 --> 00:23:51,200
it's something that we could certainly encourage our patients to do.

367
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:53,200
But what I want to highlight here

368
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:57,200
is that notice that lung is actually jumping up to number one

369
00:23:57,200 --> 00:23:59,200
as a cause of mortality.

370
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:03,200
And this sort of brings us to a point that's important to understand.

371
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:06,200
And that is one of the greatest treatments,

372
00:24:06,200 --> 00:24:08,200
or one of the greatest mechanisms

373
00:24:08,200 --> 00:24:10,200
by which we can actually handle,

374
00:24:10,200 --> 00:24:12,200
handle cancer is early detection.

375
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:15,200
If we can detect cancer early

376
00:24:15,200 --> 00:24:17,200
and remove it from the patient

377
00:24:17,200 --> 00:24:21,200
before it really can wreak havoc in that patient,

378
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:23,200
that is one of the greatest ways

379
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:25,200
by which we can serve our patients

380
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:29,200
because really the story of cancer is the story of,

381
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:34,200
sort of the best way by which to deal with a cancer

382
00:24:34,200 --> 00:24:36,200
is to actually detect it either before it becomes a cancer

383
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:38,200
and then remove it,

384
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:40,200
or to detect it before it produces clinical symptoms.

385
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:43,200
And I'm going to highlight that in a moment,

386
00:24:43,200 --> 00:24:46,200
but let's just look at this list again now

387
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:48,200
from a different perspective.

388
00:24:48,200 --> 00:24:50,200
So when you think about breast,

389
00:24:50,200 --> 00:24:52,200
we have a method of screening for breast, right?

390
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:55,200
We can do mammogram, we can do physical exam.

391
00:24:55,200 --> 00:24:58,200
Prostate, we have a mechanism for screening for prostate.

392
00:24:58,200 --> 00:24:59,200
Although it's a little bit debated,

393
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:02,200
we currently screen with PSA and digital rectal exam.

394
00:25:02,200 --> 00:25:05,200
Colorectal, we have a mechanism of screening for colorectal.

395
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:08,200
We do hemocult testing of the stool

396
00:25:08,200 --> 00:25:11,200
and we also do colonoscopy after a certain age.

397
00:25:11,200 --> 00:25:16,200
So each of these tumors can be caught relatively early.

398
00:25:16,200 --> 00:25:19,200
And because they can be caught relatively early,

399
00:25:19,200 --> 00:25:22,200
we can do a pretty good job of reducing the mortality.

400
00:25:22,200 --> 00:25:24,200
However, in the case of lung cancer,

401
00:25:24,200 --> 00:25:26,200
there is no screening mechanism.

402
00:25:26,200 --> 00:25:29,200
Lung cancer usually presents late in the course.

403
00:25:29,200 --> 00:25:34,200
There is no practical screening methodology.

404
00:25:34,200 --> 00:25:37,200
And so therefore, it pops up to number one

405
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:39,200
as far as mortality is concerned.

406
00:25:39,200 --> 00:25:42,200
And to me, one of the most interesting statistics is that

407
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:46,200
if I asked you what was the five-year survival for lung cancer

408
00:25:46,200 --> 00:25:49,200
30 years ago, the answer would be about 15%.

409
00:25:49,200 --> 00:25:53,200
And if I ask you what's the five-year survival for lung cancer today,

410
00:25:53,200 --> 00:25:55,200
the answer is about 15%.

411
00:25:55,200 --> 00:25:56,200
It hasn't changed very much.

412
00:25:56,200 --> 00:25:58,200
There's been a little bit of an increased survival

413
00:25:58,200 --> 00:26:00,200
due to improve surgical technique,

414
00:26:00,200 --> 00:26:05,200
but for the vast majority, basically, generally speaking,

415
00:26:05,200 --> 00:26:10,200
the survival of lung cancer really hasn't improved over the last 30 years.

416
00:26:10,200 --> 00:26:14,200
And that's despite the fact that we've got all these designer drugs

417
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:16,200
and understanding of molecular biology,

418
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:20,200
but I think really the underlying principle or premise there is that

419
00:26:20,200 --> 00:26:24,200
we really don't have a practical method to screen for lung cancer,

420
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:26,200
and hence we're not picking up lung cancer early.

421
00:26:26,200 --> 00:26:29,200
And because we're not picking up lung cancer early,

422
00:26:29,200 --> 00:26:33,200
we catch the cat way after it's out of the bag.

423
00:26:33,200 --> 00:26:38,200
And so, unfortunately, that's the circumstance with lung cancer,

424
00:26:38,200 --> 00:26:42,200
and this is a very important principle that I'm just trying to nail home here.

425
00:26:42,200 --> 00:26:46,200
Now, maybe it'd be a little bit...

426
00:26:46,200 --> 00:26:50,200
It's worth having a discussion of screening for a few reasons.

427
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:54,200
One is because it's tested, and two, because we have to be familiar with it.

428
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:57,200
So, it is important to sort of stop and take a step back

429
00:26:57,200 --> 00:27:00,200
and do a little discussion here on screening.

430
00:27:00,200 --> 00:27:05,200
So, let me just remind you that basically the idea in cancer is that you've got one cell,

431
00:27:05,200 --> 00:27:08,200
okay, and that one cell gets mutated.

432
00:27:08,200 --> 00:27:13,200
And that one cell, once it becomes mutated, it's then going to produce a cancer.

433
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:17,200
And this one cell will become two cells, and two cells will become four cells,

434
00:27:17,200 --> 00:27:23,200
and four cells will become eight cells, until eventually what happens is you produce a cancer

435
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:28,200
in which clinical symptoms arise, okay?

436
00:27:28,200 --> 00:27:35,200
And so, one of the things that examiners like you to know is how many divisions have to occur

437
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:39,200
before the average tumor reveals clinical symptoms.

438
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:43,200
And the answer is that there are about 30 divisions that will occur

439
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:48,200
before the earliest clinical symptoms arise in cancer.

440
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:50,200
So, that's one important point.

441
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:52,200
And that's what I've stated here.

442
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:56,200
Cancer begins as a single mutated cell, and then there's about 30 divisions

443
00:27:56,200 --> 00:27:59,200
before the earliest clinical symptoms arise.

444
00:27:59,200 --> 00:28:04,200
Now, what's important to note is every time a cancer divides, all right,

445
00:28:04,200 --> 00:28:08,200
with every division, there's going to be increased mutations

446
00:28:08,200 --> 00:28:10,200
because cancers are growing out of control.

447
00:28:10,200 --> 00:28:14,200
And so, the DNA is not being repaired, mutations are being picked up,

448
00:28:14,200 --> 00:28:18,200
checked systems are basically not able to control the cell.

449
00:28:18,200 --> 00:28:22,200
And so, with every single division, there's going to be increased mutations.

450
00:28:22,200 --> 00:28:28,200
And so, one important principle or general idea is that if you have a cancer

451
00:28:28,200 --> 00:28:31,200
that doesn't produce symptoms until very late in the disease,

452
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:36,200
it will have undergone additional mutations, sorry,

453
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:41,200
it will have undergone additional divisions which will then lead to additional mutations.

454
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:48,200
And so, whereas the average tumor or the tumor that reveals the earliest clinical symptoms

455
00:28:48,200 --> 00:28:51,200
will have divided 30 times and will then be detected,

456
00:28:51,200 --> 00:28:54,200
there are some tumors that really get detected very late.

457
00:28:54,200 --> 00:28:57,200
For example, ovarian carcinoma.

458
00:28:57,200 --> 00:29:02,200
The ovary has a large area to grow before any clinical symptoms arise,

459
00:29:02,200 --> 00:29:06,200
and because those clinical symptoms are not seen,

460
00:29:06,200 --> 00:29:09,200
and because clinical symptoms are not produced until very late,

461
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:14,200
sometimes those tumors can grow and extend far beyond these 30 divisions,

462
00:29:14,200 --> 00:29:17,200
and every time they divide, they're picking up mutations.

463
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:22,200
And so, ovarian carcinoma, generally speaking, has a pretty poor prognosis.

464
00:29:22,200 --> 00:29:24,200
Similarly, with pancreatic carcinoma.

465
00:29:24,200 --> 00:29:29,200
Pancreatic carcinoma, the pancreas has a large space to grow before clinical symptoms arise.

466
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:34,200
The pancreas has a large reserve before the tissue damage can reveal itself,

467
00:29:34,200 --> 00:29:37,200
and so pancreatic carcinoma has a horrible prognosis.

468
00:29:37,200 --> 00:29:39,200
And same thing with lung cancer.

469
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:41,200
The lung has a tremendous reserve,

470
00:29:41,200 --> 00:29:45,200
and a cancer can grow significantly before clinical symptoms actually arise.

471
00:29:45,200 --> 00:29:49,200
And so, these tumors will tend to have a worse prognosis.

472
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:51,200
And that's what I've basically stated here,

473
00:29:51,200 --> 00:29:56,200
that cancers that are detected late, they tend to have a poor prognosis.

474
00:29:56,200 --> 00:29:59,200
All right, so that's important to know.

475
00:29:59,200 --> 00:30:00,200
Now, what's the goal of screening?

476
00:30:00,200 --> 00:30:03,200
Now, remember that I told you that tumor develops from a single cell,

477
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:06,200
and that single cell becomes two cells, which becomes four cells,

478
00:30:06,200 --> 00:30:10,200
which becomes eight cells, which eventually produces clinical symptoms, right?

479
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:16,200
And every time these divisions occur, there's going to be an increase in mutations.

480
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:18,450
Okay?

481
00:30:18,450 --> 00:30:20,450
So, what is the goal of screening?

482
00:30:20,450 --> 00:30:22,450
Screening has two separate goals.

483
00:30:22,450 --> 00:30:27,450
The first goal is to catch dysplasia before it becomes cancer,

484
00:30:27,450 --> 00:30:32,450
i.e. even before this cell is present,

485
00:30:32,450 --> 00:30:38,450
we try to catch dysplasia so that we don't even have to deal with the formation of a cancer.

486
00:30:38,450 --> 00:30:39,450
What's an example?

487
00:30:39,450 --> 00:30:41,450
Well, when we do pap smear.

488
00:30:41,450 --> 00:30:46,450
When we do a pap smear, we're looking for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia,

489
00:30:46,450 --> 00:30:49,450
with CIN1, CIN2, CIN3.

490
00:30:49,450 --> 00:30:50,450
And what is CIN?

491
00:30:50,450 --> 00:30:51,450
CIN is basically dysplasia.

492
00:30:51,450 --> 00:30:52,450
It's not cancer.

493
00:30:52,450 --> 00:30:54,450
It is dysplasia.

494
00:30:54,450 --> 00:30:56,450
Now, it has a risk of developing into cancer,

495
00:30:56,450 --> 00:31:00,450
and so what we do is, when we have CIN, we remove the CIN,

496
00:31:00,450 --> 00:31:05,450
we remove the epithelium of the cervix before it can actually become cancer.

497
00:31:05,450 --> 00:31:10,450
And just note that the difference between dysplasia and cancer is that dysplasia has mutations,

498
00:31:10,450 --> 00:31:12,450
but it's reversible.

499
00:31:12,450 --> 00:31:16,450
But cancer has mutations, and it's irreversible,

500
00:31:16,450 --> 00:31:19,450
and it's growing in an unregulated manner.

501
00:31:19,450 --> 00:31:20,450
Okay?

502
00:31:20,450 --> 00:31:21,450
So, that's one goal of screening.

503
00:31:21,450 --> 00:31:24,450
It's to catch dysplasia before it becomes carcinoma.

504
00:31:24,450 --> 00:31:27,450
Another goal of screening, a separate goal of screening,

505
00:31:27,450 --> 00:31:30,450
is to detect carcinoma before clinical symptoms arise.

506
00:31:30,450 --> 00:31:35,450
So, if this is the cancer, and it's one cell, and it's going to divide into two,

507
00:31:35,450 --> 00:31:40,450
and then four, and then eight, and eventually produce clinical symptoms,

508
00:31:40,450 --> 00:31:45,450
what we'd like to do in screening is, rather than catching the tumor after it produces clinical symptoms,

509
00:31:45,450 --> 00:31:49,450
we might want to catch the tumor earlier before it produces clinical symptoms,

510
00:31:49,450 --> 00:31:51,450
and that would be a goal of screening.

511
00:31:51,450 --> 00:31:56,450
And again, the idea is, if we catch the tumor here before it produces clinical symptoms,

512
00:31:56,450 --> 00:32:01,450
we will have caught the tumor before additional mutations would be allowed to form.

513
00:32:01,450 --> 00:32:05,450
And again, the more the mutations, the more aggressive the tumor would actually be.

514
00:32:05,450 --> 00:32:11,450
So, these are the two goals of screening, and this basically comes into play in many different examples.

515
00:32:11,450 --> 00:32:14,450
For example, as I told you, what's the goal of the pap smear?

516
00:32:14,450 --> 00:32:19,450
The goal of the pap smear is to pick up cervical intraepithelial neoplasia,

517
00:32:19,450 --> 00:32:22,450
whether it be CIN1, CIN2, or CIN3.

518
00:32:22,450 --> 00:32:26,450
And the idea here is that if we pick up CIN before it becomes carcinoma,

519
00:32:26,450 --> 00:32:33,450
we will have eliminated the possibility of carcinoma developing by removing the dysplastic cells.

520
00:32:33,450 --> 00:32:37,450
Alright, so that's the goal of the pap smear.

521
00:32:37,450 --> 00:32:39,450
What's the goal of mammography?

522
00:32:39,450 --> 00:32:43,450
Well, the goal of mammography is to pick up breast cancer

523
00:32:43,450 --> 00:32:47,450
before it actually would produce clinical symptoms.

524
00:32:47,450 --> 00:32:50,450
So, actually, there's kind of two goals of mammography.

525
00:32:50,450 --> 00:32:53,450
The goal is to pick up ductal carcinoma in situ.

526
00:32:53,450 --> 00:32:55,450
What's ductal carcinoma in situ?

527
00:32:55,450 --> 00:33:02,450
That ductal carcinoma in situ is a malignant proliferation of ductal cells that grows in a duct.

528
00:33:02,450 --> 00:33:07,450
Alright, and the goal is to pick up ductal carcinoma in situ before these tumor cells invade,

529
00:33:07,450 --> 00:33:12,450
because once tumor cells invade, they now have access to the blood vessels and the lymphatic spaces,

530
00:33:12,450 --> 00:33:15,450
and there's a propensity for those tumor cells to spread.

531
00:33:15,450 --> 00:33:18,450
The more a tumor spreads, the worse the patient will actually do.

532
00:33:18,450 --> 00:33:22,450
And so one goal of mammography is to pick up ductal carcinoma in situ,

533
00:33:22,450 --> 00:33:28,450
and the idea here is that ductal carcinoma in situ, it calcifies often.

534
00:33:28,450 --> 00:33:32,450
And so mammograms will look for calcification running within ducts,

535
00:33:32,450 --> 00:33:37,450
and that's an indication that the patient may have DCIS or ductal carcinoma in situ.

536
00:33:37,450 --> 00:33:39,450
So that's one goal of mammography.

537
00:33:39,450 --> 00:33:41,450
What's another goal of mammography?

538
00:33:41,450 --> 00:33:45,450
Another goal of mammography is that if this is the patient's breast,

539
00:33:45,450 --> 00:33:52,450
generally speaking, clinically, the average clinically detected tumor,

540
00:33:52,450 --> 00:33:57,450
or tumor that's detected by a patient, the average size is 2 centimeters.

541
00:33:57,450 --> 00:33:59,450
So that's pretty big.

542
00:33:59,450 --> 00:34:02,450
However, if a patient undergoes mammography,

543
00:34:02,450 --> 00:34:06,450
the average size of detection in mammography is 1 centimeter.

544
00:34:06,450 --> 00:34:11,450
So we can pick up tumors that are 1 centimeter by mammography

545
00:34:11,450 --> 00:34:15,450
that we wouldn't be able to pick up until they became 2 centimeters clinically.

546
00:34:15,450 --> 00:34:16,450
All right?

547
00:34:16,450 --> 00:34:20,450
And so mammography allows us to detect a tumor that's half the size of that

548
00:34:20,450 --> 00:34:22,450
which would have normally been detected.

549
00:34:22,450 --> 00:34:25,450
And of course, the larger the tumor, the increase the risk of mutations.

550
00:34:25,450 --> 00:34:29,450
And generally speaking, it's known that the larger the tumor,

551
00:34:29,450 --> 00:34:34,450
the more likely it is to have spread to local lymph nodes or outside of the breast.

552
00:34:34,450 --> 00:34:40,450
And so mammography becomes an important mechanism of screening for cancer.

553
00:34:40,450 --> 00:34:45,450
Another important screening test that we can use is PSA and digital rectal exam.

554
00:34:45,450 --> 00:34:46,450
Now what's the idea here?

555
00:34:46,450 --> 00:34:50,450
The idea here is that, recall that if this is the prostate,

556
00:34:50,450 --> 00:34:53,450
and running through the center of the prostate is the urethra,

557
00:34:53,450 --> 00:35:01,450
the prostate cancer tends to grow in the posterior peripheral aspect of the prostate.

558
00:35:01,450 --> 00:35:14,450
And so it's a relatively silent disease because it doesn't really produce urinary symptoms.

559
00:35:14,450 --> 00:35:16,450
Now, the urethra runs through the center of the prostate.

560
00:35:16,450 --> 00:35:20,450
Do you know what disease process involves the center of the prostate?

561
00:35:20,450 --> 00:35:21,450
Very good, BPH.

562
00:35:21,450 --> 00:35:23,450
BPH involves the center of the prostate.

563
00:35:23,450 --> 00:35:28,450
And BPH will compress the urethra and produce urinary symptoms.

564
00:35:28,450 --> 00:35:33,450
However, prostate cancer tends to grow in the posterior periphery of the prostate

565
00:35:33,450 --> 00:35:36,450
and does not compress the urethra until very late in disease.

566
00:35:36,450 --> 00:35:39,450
And so it's a clinically silent disease.

567
00:35:39,450 --> 00:35:43,450
And so what we'd like to do is pick up these prostate cancers when they're small

568
00:35:43,450 --> 00:35:47,450
and when they have yet to spread throughout most of the prostate or outside of the prostate.

569
00:35:47,450 --> 00:35:52,450
And the way by which we can detect them is an increase in PSA or a digital rectal exam.

570
00:35:52,450 --> 00:35:56,450
Now, how does the digital rectal exam pick up prostate cancer?

571
00:35:56,450 --> 00:36:00,450
Well, recall that prostate cancer occurs in the posterior periphery of the prostate

572
00:36:00,450 --> 00:36:05,450
and running light right along the posterior periphery of the prostate is the rectum.

573
00:36:05,450 --> 00:36:11,450
And so in digital rectal exam, we get a chance to palpate the posterior periphery of the prostate

574
00:36:11,450 --> 00:36:16,450
which is exactly where prostate cancer most commonly develops.

575
00:36:16,450 --> 00:36:19,450
So this is another example of a screening test.

576
00:36:19,450 --> 00:36:25,450
And the final example that I'd like to give is the hemocult test in colonoscopy

577
00:36:25,450 --> 00:36:31,450
for detection of colonic adenomas and colonic carcinoma.

578
00:36:31,450 --> 00:36:37,450
Now the idea here is that recall that colonic carcinoma, adenocarcinoma of the colon,

579
00:36:37,450 --> 00:36:42,450
the vast majority of adenocarcinoma of the colon actually develops from adenomas.

580
00:36:42,450 --> 00:36:47,450
And this is something called the adenoma carcinoma sequence.

581
00:36:47,450 --> 00:36:54,450
We'll discuss the adenoma carcinoma sequence in more detail when we do the

582
00:36:54,450 --> 00:36:57,450
colon lecture.

583
00:36:57,450 --> 00:37:02,450
However, what I'd like to highlight here is that when a patient undergoes colonoscopy,

584
00:37:02,450 --> 00:37:04,450
the goal is to remove the adenomas.

585
00:37:04,450 --> 00:37:09,450
And if we remove the adenomas, then obviously they wouldn't be able to become carcinoma.

586
00:37:09,450 --> 00:37:11,450
And so that's one benefit of colonoscopy.

587
00:37:11,450 --> 00:37:15,450
Another benefit of colonoscopy is if the patient happens to have a cancer,

588
00:37:15,450 --> 00:37:18,450
we hope to catch the cancer before clinical symptoms are found.

589
00:37:18,450 --> 00:37:20,450
And the same with the hemocult test.

590
00:37:20,450 --> 00:37:26,450
The hemocult test has the ability to detect occult blood within the stool.

591
00:37:26,450 --> 00:37:29,450
And that occult blood could be indicative of a large adenoma,

592
00:37:29,450 --> 00:37:32,450
or it could be indicative of a carcinoma.

593
00:37:32,450 --> 00:37:35,450
And so we want to catch adenomas before they become carcinoma,

594
00:37:35,450 --> 00:37:41,450
or we'd like to catch carcinoma before it actually produces clinical symptoms.

595
00:37:41,450 --> 00:37:45,450
So these are some important examples of mechanisms of screening.

596
00:37:45,450 --> 00:37:50,450
And hopefully by now you've got the general idea in your mind of the benefits of screening.

597
00:37:50,450 --> 00:37:54,450
And that really closes off this very introductory discussion of neoplasia.

598
00:37:54,450 --> 00:37:56,450
Much of what I said is high yield.

599
00:37:56,450 --> 00:38:00,450
Much of what I said is going to apply to the organ systems,

600
00:38:00,450 --> 00:38:04,450
the particular organ systems in which they would normally be discussed.

601
00:38:04,450 --> 00:38:08,450
For example, colonoscopy, PSA, digital rectal exam, et cetera.

602
00:38:08,450 --> 00:38:12,450
But what we'll do here is take a moment to, we'll stop here,

603
00:38:12,450 --> 00:38:14,450
give you some time to digest the material,

604
00:38:14,450 --> 00:38:19,450
and when you're ready we can continue with the discussion of carcinogenesis.

605
00:38:19,450 --> 00:38:35,450
Sometimes we'll reduce them in the developing phenomena.

606
00:38:35,450 --> 00:38:39,450
However, the ones that reward them in the protection of carcinogenesis

607
00:38:39,450 --> 00:38:40,450
are important.

